\title{sequnit: Sequential Monitoring of Randomised Unit Tests}
\description{
Monitors randomised unit tests across successive code changes with a
non-restarting cumulative sum (CUSUM) chart. The package builds on
testthat. Each call of \code{\link[=expect_pval]{expect_pval()}} updates one chart by one user commit and
appends the result to the chart's log. The chart signals, and the test fails,
when the CUSUM statistic ends a commit at or above the threshold \code{h}.
}
\details{
The package also provides a ready-made GitHub Actions pipeline:
\code{\link[=workflow_gen]{workflow_gen()}} writes a workflow that runs the tests of a repository or
package on pushes, on a schedule or by hand. It keeps the logs between runs
on a branch of the repository or as a workflow artifact depending on permissions.
Logs read with \code{\link[=read_pval_log]{read_pval_log()}} can be printed and plotted, see
\code{\link[=plot.cusum_log]{plot.cusum_log()}}.
}
\section{The chart}{

Each p-value \eqn{p} adds the increment \eqn{\Phi^{-1}(1 - p) - \delta/2}
to the CUSUM statistic \eqn{S}, which is kept within \eqn{[0, H]}. Here
\eqn{\delta} is the shift in the mean of \eqn{\Phi^{-1}(1 - p)} that the
test detects with probability \code{power} at level \code{alpha}. Within a commit,
further tests are run while \eqn{S \ge v}, up to \code{amax} in total. The
chart is designed by \code{\link[=cusum_design]{cusum_design()}}.
}

\section{Options}{

\describe{
